      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Genus-level entry: azalea hybrids (small, indoor or garden) and rhododendrons (large shrubs) share one entry; scientific name is the genus. Needs acid soil or ericaceous compost: RHS says moist but well-drained acid soil, pH 5.0 to 6.0, rich in organic matter; on alkaline soil grow in containers. Indoor forced azalea (R. simsii, RHS indoor page) is frost tender and wants a cool humid place, never dry; outdoor types mostly fully hardy, dappled shade. Watering: keep_moist is the agent's mapping, 4 days is a guess. Sources differ in plant: the RHS guide is the outdoor genus, the RHS indoor page is R. simsii, the NC State page is R. indicum; no NC State genus page was found. Size: RHS says 'variable' (indoor azalea 45 to 60 cm), NC State R. indicum 60 to 90 cm; not stored. Growth slow: NC State R. indicum only. Pots: loam-based ericaceous compost, repot every other year (RHS). Toxicity: ASPCA lists azalea (Rhododendron spp.) toxic to dogs, cats and horses: grayanotoxin, vomiting, diarrhoea, weakness, cardiac failure; severity 3 is our grading (cardiac failure named). NC State grades R. indicum 'low severity' ('moderate amounts pose little hazard'), a conflict with ASPCA; the higher grade is kept for pets. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
